              "textField": "Many of the commonly seen ‘lung pathologies’ are actually due to something outside the lungs and as such, we will cover those pathologies in another module. For instance, in a *pulmonary embolism* a blood clot from the deep veins in the legs breaks off, going into the right side of the heart and ultimately getting lodged in a *pulmonary artery*. Since the lungs get their fresh oxygen from a different source, many of these do not result in immediate death of cells. In a *pleural effusion*, fluid builds up in the membranous sac that surrounds the lungs, often due to *congestive heart failure* and greatly decreasing the amount of the lung that can exchange gasses. In *pleuritis*, there is an inflammation in the *pleural sac*, which can turn into a clinical emergency fairly quickly, and must be treated with intravenously administered antibiotics."
